#5) If this gene is knocked out, the expected phenotype in mice would involve severe ocular abnormalities. Loss of Pax6 function leads to defects ranging from microphthalmia (reduced eye size) to anophthalmia (complete absence of one or both eyes). This phenotype is observed in “small eye” (Sey) mice, which carry mutations in Pax6. Pax6 is a key transcription factor required for early eye development, including lens formation and retinal differentiation. In its absence, the lens often fails to form, and retinal development is disrupted. This can result in altered proliferation and differentiation of retinal progenitor cells, leading to abnormal proportions or loss of specific neuronal cell types within the retina.